Z||000000||Master_Sushi||06-27-2000||11:05 PM||||well as you might know I got a new fiero recently. As I stated its in "almost" perfect contition. Well this is the almost  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Its the biggest prob with it, it seems that the dash is coming apart from the under part. Its probably a common ware problum, and easy to fix.But... thats about as well as I can explain it <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">, so I enclosed a picture. <P>Thanks SO much for the help, having this fixed will make me very happy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ConRip.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||11
Z||000001||Phil||06-28-2000||08:13 AM||||Super glue gel||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||06-28-2000||09:03 AM||||The easiest repair is to replace the console panel.  Check with the suppliers of used Fiero parts as to cost.  It may be cheaper than you think.  What also happens is the console curls up where it meets the carpet and gluing it back into position is normally a lost cause.<P>I think there are four screws that hold that panel in place located under the ashtrays. You probably will need to remove the shift knob (see Haynes manual) to pull the panel off.||||reg||7
Z||000003||tgowens||06-28-2000||10:22 AM||||the trim bezel is held on by two 7mm screws located under each ashtray. The shift knob can be removed by removing the horseshoe clip from the front of the handle and lifting it off, then the bezel may be lifted off. There is a small light bulb attached to the underside of the gear selector indicator, twist to remove. -- the method I use to repair the curled vinyl around the console parts is to apply 3M glue (a super-glue type) to the pieces, flatten them with paint sticks and clamp them for a few minutes......I have done this with all of my console pieces with wonderful results.......they look like new. -- also, when replacing the center console trim bezel, remember to line up the gear selector indicator properly (it fits down over the gear selector) and reattaching the light before remounting it........and, DON'T LOSE THAT HORSESHOE CLIP!! <p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 06-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Master_Sushi||06-28-2000||10:10 PM||||Ok, thanks for all the advice. I think I am going to try opening it up and super-glueing it. If that dosent work to well I will just replace it all.<P>Thanks again for all the help! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>this forum is the best  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><P>3 cheers for cliff!||||reg||1
